<sn><md>### Example: Local to Remote

Suppose you have:

```text
/home/emon/Projects
├── project1
│   └── file.txt
└── project2
    └── notes.txt
```

Run:

```bash
rclone copy ~/Projects gdrive:Backup
```

The result will be:

```text
gdrive:
└── Backup
    ├── project1
    │   └── file.txt
    └── project2
        └── notes.txt
```

Notice that `rclone copy` copies the **contents** of the source directory into the destination.

</md></sn><sn><md>### Remote to Local 

You can use `rclone copy` to copy files from a cloud remote, such as Google Drive, to a folder on your computer.

**Example**
Suppose your Google Drive contains:

```text
gdrive:
└── Backup
    ├── notes.txt
    ├── photo.jpg
    └── University
        └── assignment.pdf
```

Run:

```bash
rclone copy gdrive:Backup ~/Downloads/Backup
```

The result will be:

```text
~/Downloads/Backup/
├── notes.txt
├── photo.jpg
└── University/
    └── assignment.pdf
```

If the local destination folder does not exist, Rclone will generally create the required directory structure.

+++ **Copy a Single File**

```bash
rclone copyto gdrive:Documents/notes.pdf ~/Downloads/notes.pdf
```

`copyto` is useful when copying **one specific file** to an exact destination.

+++ 

`rclone copy` does **not delete extra files** from the destination.


#### Recommended Command

Before copying a large amount of data:

```bash
rclone copy --dry-run gdrive:Backup ~/Backup
```

This previews what Rclone would copy.

Then run:

```bash
rclone copy -P gdrive:Backup ~/Backup
```

The `-P` option displays transfer progress.


**Useful Options**

| Command                                        | Purpose                        |
| ---------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------ |
| `rclone copy gdrive:Backup ~/Backup`           | Copy remote → local            |
| `rclone copyto gdrive:file.txt ~/file.txt`     | Copy one file to an exact path |
| `rclone copy --dry-run gdrive:Backup ~/Backup` | Preview without copying        |
| `rclone copy -P gdrive:Backup ~/Backup`        | Show progress                  |

</md></sn><sn><md>### Creating Nested Directories

You can specify a complete destination path:

```bash
rclone copy ~/Documents gdrive:Backup/2026/September/Documents
```

Rclone will create the necessary destination directories automatically.

```text
gdrive:
└── Backup
    └── 2026
        └── September
            └── Documents
```

+++ **Important: `copy` Does Not Always Copy the Source Folder Itself**

Consider:

```bash
rclone copy ~/Documents gdrive:Backup
```

The result is generally:

```text
gdrive:Backup/
├── file1.txt
├── file2.pdf
└── Study/
```

The `Documents` folder itself is **not automatically created as an additional folder level**.

If you specifically want to copy the folder's contents into a folder named `Documents`, use:

```bash
rclone copy ~/Documents gdrive:Backup/Documents
```

Result:

```text
gdrive:Backup/
└── Documents/
    ├── file1.txt
    ├── file2.pdf
    └── Study/
```

+++

</md></sn><sn><md>### Creating an Empty Directory

`rclone copy` copies files, so it may not create a completely empty source directory.

To explicitly create a directory, use:

```bash
rclone mkdir gdrive:NewFolder
```

For nested directories:

```bash
rclone mkdir gdrive:Backup/2026/September
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### Quick Reference

| Task                                            | Command                                          |
| ----------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |
| Copy files and create destination automatically | `rclone copy source remote:destination`          |
| Create an empty directory                       | `rclone mkdir remote:folder`                     |
| Copy local folder contents                      | `rclone copy ~/Folder remote:Destination`        |
| Copy into a specifically named remote folder    | `rclone copy ~/Folder remote:Destination/Folder` |
</md></sn>